A||||7||rodmcneill||Installing a hitch||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||11-30-2000||10:42 AM||||Since my Fiero has become my daily driver(no complaints)I was wondering how difficult it is to install a hitch on a Fiero? I would like to install a 1 1/4" receiver hitch primarily to carry 2 bikes. In some of the catalogs I have seen they have them for coupes but not GT models, why is this? They all have the same frame (84-87). Is there a big enough difference in the rear fascia that makes it unfittable on GTs?||||reg||1
Z||000001||007DOUG||11-30-2000||11:09 AM||||I've got both the coupe and GT style hithces installed. There's a huge difference.<P>Either one is easy to install. They use two existing holes, and requires drilling two additional holes. <P>GT bumpers are lower to the ground when compared to coupe bumpers. Therefore the hitch is lower. You'll never install a coupe hitch on a GT unless you like cutting a hole in your bumper.<P>My coupe hitch has a fixed tongue and can't tow over 1500#, where my GT hitch has a receiver and tows other cars. <P>I use my bike rack on either hitch, though the coupe requires an adapter.<P>------------------<BR>5 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>84 SE<BR>86 SE V6 153K<BR>86 GT<BR>86 GT now with a 4:10<BR>86 GT 3.4 Pearl White T-top<BR>2 88 GT's (twins)<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||11-30-2000||11:30 AM||||007, whats the heaviest thing you've towed and for how far? ever with a 4 cyl.? i'd like to maybe get one to be able to tow a small trailer and dirt bike.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rodmcneill||11-30-2000||12:35 PM||||007Doug Where did you buy your hitch for the GT and what brand? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||007DOUG||11-30-2000||11:26 PM||||Coupe hitch was bought from 1-800-hitches. The GT hitch was bought from The Fiero Store. I have no idea who manufactured each.<P>The heaviest tow: another fiero <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierotow.jpg">  <BR>400+ miles home. Using my stock 153K mile V6 SE auto. I drove the PA turnpike home from NJ. Two monster hills on the pike, and she climbed at 60mph. Got a horrible 17mpg.<P>I suppose a 4 banger would pull dirtbikes, wouldn't suggest alot of hill climbing. I only use my 4 banger hitch as a bike rack. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||horseman||11-30-2000||11:59 PM||||Hey 007Doug.<BR>That is an impressive collection of fiero's!<BR>How about posting some more pics? Especially of the Indy's.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Charlie||||reg||6
Z||000006||Hank is Here||12-01-2000||12:12 AM||||007DOUG, <BR>nothing personal but what the he!! were you thinking.   you said you went up the hills at 60mph, now how was the other end gonig down them?  How did the 4 wheel disc work with a double load on them?||||reg||1
Z||000007||007DOUG||12-01-2000||09:27 AM||||Yeah, I know, I know. I normally transport fieros with my flatbed trailer and suburban. But I wanted to try this just once. I've heard it could be done. I think Mr. Fiero has towed alot of fieros home this way. Perhaps he'll share an expierence or two <P>Kept my foot off the gas going down hill, occasionally had to use brakes. I won't even mention how fast I was going on flat ground. I had a brand spankin new U-haul dolly, and it was the best pulling dolly ever. It was really wide, very stable.<P>I towed at night, so not alot of "other drivers" to "watch out for". Takes plenty of extra real estate to stop.<P>horseman: Pics/description of fiero collection is found my visitng "My Garage" on my website. ||||reg||1
